9. Testing the UI Policy

The UI Policy should be tested using different Incident form scenarios.

Test Case 1: Form Load

Action:

Open or create an Incident record.
Observe the configured fields.

Expected Result:

The Incident form loads successfully.
UI Policy behavior is applied according to the configured condition.
Test Case 2: Condition Becomes True

Action:

Change the Incident field values so that the UI Policy condition is satisfied.

Expected Result:

The configured UI Policy Actions are applied.
Required fields become mandatory where configured.
Configured fields become read-only where applicable.
Visibility changes are applied where configured.
Test Case 3: Condition Becomes False

Action:

Change the Incident values so that the UI Policy condition is no longer satisfied.

Expected Result:

The UI Policy is no longer active.
Fields return to their normal behavior when Reverse if false is configured.
Test Case 4: Mandatory Field Validation

Action:

Leave a field configured as mandatory without a value.
Attempt to submit the Incident.

Expected Result:

The system prevents submission until the required information is provided.
Test Case 5: Read-only Field

Action:

Activate the UI Policy condition.
Attempt to modify a field configured as read-only.

Expected Result:

The field cannot be manually modified while the UI Policy condition is active.
